  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/assets/3b53e88e4f93985ca999c7fd9d4e3fe3591afe10e7fbe499a303d22bfb0ce6ea.png)\n\nFor the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{x+4}+1$ shown above, what is the expression for the area $A$ as a definite integral?\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $A$ | $=$ | $\\int $ | {{field-2}} | $\\text{d}x$ |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} |  |  |",
  "worked_solution": "We want to express the area $A$ between the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{x+4}+1$ and the $x$-axis, between $x=-3$ and $x=2.$\n\nThis can be expressed as the definite integral of $f(x)=\\sqrt{x+4}+1$ between the lower limit $x=-3$ and the upper limit $x=2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-3}^{2}\\sqrt{x+4}+1\\,\\text{d}x\n$$",
